<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>LocalScribe.Onnx</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../LocalScribe.Core/LocalScribe.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The QNN package carries the Qualcomm AI Engine Direct libraries and only ships for
    win-arm64. Referencing it unconditionally would break builds and CI on every other
    platform, so it is brought in only when building for Windows on ARM. On other platforms
    the QNN provider simply fails to register and the planner falls back, which is the
    behaviour we want anyway.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <UseQnnRuntime Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'win-arm64' Or '$(Platform)' == 'arm64'">true</UseQnnRuntime>
    <UseQnnRuntime Condition="'$(UseQnnRuntime)' == ''">false</UseQnnRuntime>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    These two are alternatives, never companions. Both ship their own
    runtimes/win-arm64/native/onnxruntime.dll, and when both are referenced the base package's
    copy wins the file conflict. That build looks correct — the Qnn*.dll helper libraries are
    all present — but the runtime beside them was compiled without the QNN provider, so it
    never registers and every session silently falls back to the CPU. The QNN package depends
    on Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Managed, so it brings the managed assembly with it and nothing
    is lost by dropping the base reference on arm64.
  -->
  <!--
    Pinned deliberately. Precompiled QNN context binaries are tied to the QAIRT and ONNX Runtime
    versions they were built against, and a mismatch fails at load with "Failed to create context
    from binary" — which names neither version. 1.22.0 is what the published large-v3-turbo build
    for X Elite asks for; 1.20.1 got as far as opening the file and no further.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseQnnRuntime)' == 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.QNN" Version="1.22.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The non-QNN branch floats ahead of the QNN pin on purpose. Nothing here loads a context
    binary, so the 1.22.0 compatibility reason above does not apply — and 1.22.0's macOS build
    aborts in its native teardown at process exit (mutex lock failed in libc++), which 1.29
    fixes. The two branches never meet in one process: a build is either win-arm64 with QNN or
    everything-else without it. Any bump here is judged the project's way: the doctor's
    diarize turn diff and align stride check before and after, because the diarization tuning
    is frozen and a runtime that moves a boundary by a hundredth of a second is a tuning
    change.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseQnnRuntime)' != 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime" Version="1.29.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
